# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Margin Left Blank After Six Strings

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 84 BPM. Selam opens with a five-note krar cell repeated three times, then stops for two full bars. Jonah refuses to fill the first bar entirely. In the second, one filtered snare fragment appears late, followed by a short shelf-wheel tape loop. Mekdes enters only after the second repetition with a prepared-organ open fifth that occupies half of the silence and cuts before the krar returns.

Noah adds one breath-heavy bass-trombone note during the third Shadow Interval, beginning without a clean attack and settling into pitch just before Selam’s next cycle. Dawit replaces the following programmed snare with one fingertip frame-drum slap.

At the midpoint, the krar phrase remains unchanged but each Shadow Interval becomes shorter by one eighth note. The surrounding material therefore compresses without any increase in BPM. The final phrase ends normally, but the expected electronic response is omitted. Four seconds of room tone close the track.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cream archival page with six short black string-like marks near the left edge and a large untouched blank margin, krar bridge, green desk lamp, and reel-to-reel leader tape in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “leave the margin,” “six strings stop,” “nothing in the first bar,” and “let the room answer.”

## Track 2: Drawer 27 Hums Behind the Pluck

**Story & Context:** At 91 BPM, Jonah builds the EP’s most active beat from short kick, clipped snare, paper-cutter knock, and a two-beat ventilation loop. Selam plays a brighter krar ostinato in a high register, but every fourth phrase ends one note early. That missing final note creates a larger Shadow Interval than the listener expects.

Mekdes answers only the shortened phrases with a low pump-organ suspended second. Noah plays a muted descending fourth from inside the ventilation noise, while Dawit removes alternating electronic kicks and replaces them with soft palm bass strokes.

A metal archive drawer labeled 27 is recorded resonating after being gently closed. The single hum is used only at major structural turns, never looped continuously. In the final minute, Jonah removes the ventilation bed entirely. The groove suddenly feels brighter even though no new instrument enters.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Steel archive drawer marked 27 slightly open beneath a green desk lamp, krar string shadow crossing the metal face, waveform of one short drawer resonance on a paper note card. Spoken fragments limited to “drawer twenty-seven,” “one note missing,” “hum behind it,” and “remove the air.”

## Track 3: Ink Roller Turns After Everyone Leaves

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 78 BPM with Selam alone, playing widely spaced low krar dyads. Each phrase is followed by a four-bar Shadow Interval. Mekdes sustains faint prepared-organ reeds in the first gap, Noah contributes only breath in the second, and Dawit adds one brushed frame-drum stroke in the third. Jonah’s beat does not enter until nearly two minutes into the track.

When the drums arrive, they remain skeletal: one dry kick, one clipped snare, and a low sub note every second bar. The krar never becomes busier. Instead, the surrounding tape loops accumulate—paper movement, reel motor, ventilation, and a mechanical ink roller recorded turning by hand.

During the final section, the musicians disappear in reverse order. Drums stop, trombone leaves, organ closes, frame drum falls silent, and Selam plays one last open fifth. The only remaining sound is the ink roller making three slow revolutions before the tape cuts.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty print archive after rehearsal, one old ink roller turning beneath a narrow green lamp, krar case closed on a wooden table, loose cream paper and dark tape leader nearby. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “everyone left,” “roller still turning,” “three revolutions,” and “cut before the fourth.”

